  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 233 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A few rain showers continue across the region this hour, \nbut will gradually diminish through the morning. Although an \nisolated shower or two may be possible today, especially across the \nhigh terrain, dry weather is expected to prevail. Temperatures today \nwill be on the cooler side, generally in the upper 60s to low 70s. \nClearing skies will allow for efficient radiational cooling tonight, \nwith lows falling into the 40s and 50s, with some of the colder \nhollows dropping into the upper 30s. A brief period of ridging looks \nto build in for Monday, continuing to bring mostly dry conditions \nthroughout the day. A weak cold front pushing through Monday night \nmay bring spark some light rain showers, particularly near the \nCanadian border.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A rather vigorous shortwave trough will be speeding \nthrough the longwave pattern Wednesday night and Thursday. This \nfeature is expected to entrain an impressive plume of moisture from \nthe Gulf with PWAT values surging to 2+ inches (2-3 standard \ndeviations above normal). This will allow a period of moderate to \nheavy rainfall to move across the region during the overnight hours. \nHowever, moist adiabatic lapse profiles up to 200 mb leave much to be \ndesired in terms of instability which should inhibit any \nthunderstorms potential, Thus, we expect more of a beneficial \nrainfall event with the hydrological issue appear unlikely at this \ntime. A strong 100+ knot 500 mb jet and 30-45 kt LLJ will help to \nquickly push this system to our east with most of the rainfall \nexiting the region by daybreak on Thursday. \n\nA broad upper level trough will remain across the region Thursday \nand Friday which could set the stage for some isolated to scattered \nafternoon showers but additional rainfall will be light as the deep \nplume of moisture will be shunted eastward. Global deterministic and \nensemble guidance are coming into better agreement in showing that \nmid-level height rises appear likely late Friday into next weekend \nwhich should bring drier conditions back to the region.",
